{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Now the priest of Midian had seven daughters.</b> Scripture does not mention his name, for he was not known except by the title with which he was honored in his priesthood. He is Jethro, for after Moses married into his family it is written, \"and he returned to Jethro his father-in-law\" (Exodus 4:18), and there it is written, \"and Jethro said to Moses, Go in peace,\" like Elijah (II Kings 1:3) and Elijahu (I Kings 17:1), Jeremiah (Jeremiah 27:1) and Jeremiyahu (ibid. 1:1). After he converted he was called Hobab, as it is written, \"of the children of Hobab, Moses' father-in-law\" (Judges 4:11), for it is the way of all who become Jews to take for themselves another name in Israel. He is the son of Reuel, as it is written, \"and Moses said to Hobab the son of Reuel the Midianite\" (Numbers 10:29). What is said here (Exodus 2:18), \"and they came to Reuel their father,\" means the father of their father, like \"the God of my father Abraham\" (Genesis 32:10), \"Nebuchadnezzar his father\" (Daniel 5:2), and likewise \"do you know Laban the son of Nahor\" (Genesis 29:5), and likewise \"Mephibosheth the son of Saul\" (II Samuel 19:25), and many like these. The reason for this was that the priest was not to be found in the house, for he had been driven from his priesthood in the house of his god, and they came to the old man. And it is possible that \"Moses consented to dwell with the man\" (below, verse 21) refers to the priest mentioned above, for he is the one who gave him Zipporah his daughter:" "<b>And they came and drew.</b> For the shepherds would come every day and fill the troughs and water their flocks first, and afterward these women would water theirs. It happened that on this day the women came first — <b>and they came and drew</b> — for they intended to water their flocks first before the shepherds arrived, but behold, <b>the shepherds came and drove them away</b> from the troughs to water first, according to their custom every day. Moses was incensed at the injustice and saved them, for since they had already filled the troughs, the water was theirs. <b>And also drew for them</b>, because the troughs did not suffice for all their flock. This is the reason for <b>why did you come so quickly today</b>, and they said <b>an Egyptian man saved us from the hand of the shepherds</b> — for they drive us away every single day whenever we come to the troughs first:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :register {:flags {0 ["God"]}}, :notes {0 "The phrase בבית אלהיו, rendered hyper-literally 'in the house of his god,' could refer to a pagan temple; the passage is left as Ramban wrote it without specifying whether 'his god' is singular or plural."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:23.939826672Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}